WEBVTT

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.120
In this video, I want to tell you

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.610
about the story of my English learning journey.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.510
I went from understanding nothing in English to getting my C2 level certificate.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.130
IELTS band score 9, Cambridge CELTA grade A.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.290
But how did it happen?

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.370
I am pretty sure my story has a lot to teach you.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.850
Because I am a non-native English speaker.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.620
English is not my first language.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.870
In fact, it's my third.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.800
So how did I learn English?

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.250
It all started when I was eight years old.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.060
Back then, I remember clearly one day, a friend of mine,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.800
a classmate, came, sat next to me and said,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.860
"Did you know that this creature is called a fish in English?"

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.060
That word was very, very strange.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.620
The whole concept of a new language was very weird to me.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.520
I did not know the alphabet of English.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.340
I did not know anything about the language.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.860
And so one day, my parents told

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.700
me that we want you to learn English

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.010
because it's useful for your future.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.820
I did not have a very specific goal.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.160
I was eight years old.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.250
I wanted to go out and play with my friends.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.150
But my parents said, "It's something useful."

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.240
And actually, right now, I'm very grateful for what they did.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.180
So they said, "You need to learn English."

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.216
And back then, there was only one option:

00:01:30.526 --> 00:01:32.666
go to a language school and sign up.

00:01:32.666 --> 00:01:35.546
And so I did.

00:01:35.546 --> 00:01:39.086
From the age of eight to the age of 12,

00:01:39.086 --> 00:01:44.686
for four years, I went to a language school following traditional books.

00:01:44.686 --> 00:01:48.126
There's a teacher, there are classmates, there are students,

00:01:48.126 --> 00:01:51.656
you sit down, and you do as the teacher instructs.

00:01:52.806 --> 00:01:57.166
That was when I didn't learn English.

00:01:57.166 --> 00:01:58.526
What do I mean?

00:01:58.526 --> 00:02:01.566
Four years? But did I really study?

00:02:01.566 --> 00:02:04.186
No, I was just there to have fun.

00:02:04.186 --> 00:02:07.096
I was just there to play with my new friends.

00:02:07.966 --> 00:02:14.726
Back then, there was a game center right across from the language school.

00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:18.126
And I remember clearly, my parents would drop me off

00:02:18.126 --> 00:02:22.196
in front of the school and I would go inside.

00:02:22.706 --> 00:02:26.696
But then I would wait and check and see if they've left or not.

00:02:26.926 --> 00:02:29.226
And when I saw that they had left,

00:02:29.226 --> 00:02:32.946
I would walk back and go across the street and

00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:36.026
go to the game center and sit there with my friends.

00:02:36.026 --> 00:02:40.056
And instead of being at school, we would play games together.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.616
That's what happened for the major part of the four years.

00:02:43.906 --> 00:02:49.466
Only one time, the principal of the school called my parents and said,

00:02:49.466 --> 00:02:54.006
"Hey, after 20 sessions, your son has only attended eight."

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:58.746
And I was like, "Wow." Yeah, we had a fight.

00:02:58.746 --> 00:03:02.622
Back then, it was a summer and so I stopped going to school.

00:03:03.112 --> 00:03:05.112
But during the summer that I took

00:03:05.112 --> 00:03:08.622
a break from going to school, what happened?

00:03:09.312 --> 00:03:11.492
This is probably the turning point.

00:03:11.492 --> 00:03:12.592
Very important.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.832
I want you to pay attention.

00:03:13.832 --> 00:03:17.772
I started liking two people, Nicolas Cage,

00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:21.032
American actor, and Eminem, American rapper.

00:03:21.032 --> 00:03:27.682
And I started watching Nicolas Cage movies and listening to Eminem's songs.

00:03:28.152 --> 00:03:31.512
Why? Because I wanted to learn English? No.

00:03:31.512 --> 00:03:32.982
Because that was my hobby.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:37.482
I was doing it because I enjoyed doing it.

00:03:38.232 --> 00:03:43.622
I remember back then, Nicolas Cage, 2009, had a movie called Knowing.

00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:48.082
It was a sci-fi movie about aliens, about the end of the world.

00:03:48.572 --> 00:03:51.332
And I was always fascinated by that topic.

00:03:51.332 --> 00:03:53.912
So I started watching that movie and

00:03:53.912 --> 00:03:56.142
I really enjoyed watching it the first time.

00:03:56.592 --> 00:04:00.972
But then I had another idea, because Nicolas Cage was my role model,

00:04:00.972 --> 00:04:03.492
and I wanted to act like him,

00:04:03.492 --> 00:04:07.492
and I wanted to be like him, and I wanted to sound like him.

00:04:07.492 --> 00:04:10.252
I started watching the video

00:04:10.252 --> 00:04:14.552
without the subtitle, playing the video,

00:04:14.552 --> 00:04:21.032
listening to one line, pausing the video and writing down what I understood.

00:04:21.032 --> 00:04:26.272
Some sentences were difficult, so I would go back and listen several times,

00:04:26.272 --> 00:04:30.292
and even then, I may have not understood something,

00:04:30.292 --> 00:04:31.767
so I would leave it blank.

00:04:32.577 --> 00:04:34.617
By the end of that movie,

00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:38.867
do you know how many pages of transcript I had?

00:04:38.997 --> 00:04:41.907
This much paper, very thick.

00:04:42.177 --> 00:04:44.867
A4 paper, front and back.

00:04:45.337 --> 00:04:47.497
And it took me about two months,

00:04:47.497 --> 00:04:50.267
two months and a half to finish the movie.

00:04:50.677 --> 00:04:53.877
But I did it not because I wanted to learn English,

00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:55.767
but because I was having fun.

00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:57.957
Or let's talk about Eminem.

00:04:57.957 --> 00:05:03.157
Back then, I was really into Eminem's rap, not generally rap, but Eminem's rap.

00:05:03.157 --> 00:05:08.757
And I would listen to his music and try to memorize the lines.

00:05:08.757 --> 00:05:10.157
I wasn't transcribing.

00:05:10.157 --> 00:05:14.827
This time, I was memorizing and reading out loud, trying to follow him.

00:05:15.317 --> 00:05:17.037
And you know, when you were singing hip hop,

00:05:17.037 --> 00:05:21.017
when you're singing rap, your tongue has to move a lot,

00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:24.347
your jaw has to move a lot, and your brain has to keep up.

00:05:24.617 --> 00:05:29.057
So, listening to Eminem's songs trained my ear.

00:05:29.057 --> 00:05:31.717
Repeating after him trained my jaw and my

00:05:31.717 --> 00:05:35.327
tongue and my speaking muscles and my brain.

00:05:35.657 --> 00:05:39.537
And so, after that summer, I went back to the language school.

00:05:39.537 --> 00:05:41.787
But this time, something was different.

00:05:42.677 --> 00:05:46.037
This time, I was the best student in that class.

00:05:46.037 --> 00:05:49.397
This time, I could easily talk to other people.

00:05:49.397 --> 00:05:51.997
This time, I got the highest grades.

00:05:51.997 --> 00:05:54.797
This time, people would ask me questions,

00:05:54.797 --> 00:05:58.347
even though I was the youngest in that class.

00:05:58.477 --> 00:06:02.587
This time, I knew how to speak English.
